<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Turnip Winter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Steckrübenwinter</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Turnip Winter was the severe food crisis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the winter of 1916–1917, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, when a failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> harvest and the British naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> left many civilians depending on turnips, normally used as animal fodder. Hunger, disease and malnutrition killed many people and badly damaged civilian morale and trust in the government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turnip-winter.jpg" alt="Turnip Winter" width="273" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stadt Erfurt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kohlr%C3%BCbenkarte_stadt_erfurt_1917.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> entered the war in the summer of 1914 facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on two fronts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlieffen-plan">Schlieffen Plan</a>, meant to defeat France quickly, failed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-the-marne">First Battle of the Marne</a> in September, and by the end of 1914 the Western Front had settled into trench warfare. Towards the end of that year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> blockaded the northern approaches to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> to cut off supplies to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>. Germany could produce most of the industrial needs of the war but not enough food.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Poor weather reduced the harvest, above all of cereals, and a poor autumn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> harvest left much produce rotting in the fields. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, the army's seizure of horses, the conscription of farm workers and a shortage of fertiliser, as nitrogen went into explosives, all added to the shortfall. The government had founded the War Food Office on 22 May 1916 to run rationing. In the summer of 1917 the ration gave only 1,560 calories a day, falling to 1,000 in winter, against the 3,000 the Imperial Health Office set for a healthy adult man.</p><p>The historian Avner Offer suggests that one-fifth to one-third of food could be had only on the black market. Hungry children broke into barns and stripped orchards, and youth crime roughly doubled. The historian G. J. Meyer cites a prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> physician's report that eighty thousand children had died of starvation in 1916. Food shortages led to strikes, the best known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> in the summer of 1917, where workers complained that food was shared out unevenly. Female mortality rose by 11.5% in 1916 and by 30% in 1917 compared with pre-war rates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The hardship showed Germans how hard-pressed the country had become and badly affected morale. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">Potatoes</a> had been the food Germans ate most, and the switch to the Swedish turnip, or rutabaga, normally fed to animals, changed their tastes and deprived them of the vitamins and minerals they were used to. Kriegsbrot, the war bread made with potato flour, became much harder to digest once substitutes replaced the potato flour. Evelyn Blücher, an English woman married to Prince Blücher, wrote that the rounded contours of the German nation had become a legend of the past. There were social consequences too, such as the pillaging of food stores after the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Turnip Winter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British naval blockade caused severe food shortages, worst in the <b>Turnip Winter</b> of 1916 to 1917, and wore down civilian morale faster than any battlefield defeat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
